5	Our study uses a single architecture (OpenVLA-OFT, LoRA rank 32) in MuJoCo simulation on SafeLIBERO Level~I (10 tasks); generalization to other task distributions, real-world deployment, and dynamic obstacles remains open. The collision metric is binary (L1 threshold $10^{-3}$), which does not capture collision severity or near-miss events. The factorial uses $n{=}3$--$4$ seeds per cell, yielding estimated power of ${\approx}0.35$--$0.45$ for detecting large effects ($d{\geq}0.8$); accordingly, no pairwise comparison reaches significance at $\alpha{=}0.05$ with Bonferroni correction, and we rely on effect sizes as the primary inferential quantity. The ANOVA results should be interpreted as exploratory rather than confirmatory; future replication with $n{\geq}8$ per cell would be needed to achieve adequate power (${\geq}0.80$). The auxiliary loss weight ($\lambda_0{=}0.01$) and its exponential decay schedule were not swept; different weighting schemes could alter the gradient flow effect magnitude. The BatchNorm running-statistics hypothesis is mechanistically plausible but not directly verified (e.g., via frozen statistics or train/eval mode comparison); the content irrelevance finding was observed under v1 and requires v2 replication. All experiments use LoRA adapters; full fine-tuning may exhibit different sensitivity to the dual-objective landscape. The codebase sensitivity finding (Section~5) implies that our quantitative results may not transfer to other VLA implementations without controlled replication.
